<h2>Why the L-Shape Kitchen Dominates Indian Homes</h2>
<p>Walk into any modern Indian apartment — whether it is a 2BHK in Electronic City or a 3BHK in Whitefield — and you will find some version of the L-shape layout. It is not a coincidence. The L-shape kitchen is the most space-efficient layout for rooms between 60 and 150 square feet, which covers the vast majority of Indian residential kitchens.</p>
<p>The layout uses two adjacent walls, forming an "L" that creates a natural work triangle between the stove, sink, and refrigerator. The open side invites movement, allows a dining counter or breakfast bar, and keeps the cook connected to the living area.</p>
<h3>Advantages Over Other Layouts</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Corner utilisation:</strong> Converts the dead corner — which straight kitchens waste entirely — into usable storage with carousel units, magic corners, or Le Mans pull-outs.</li>
  <li><strong>Work triangle efficiency:</strong> The L-shape naturally creates a compact triangle between wash, cook, and store zones. Steps between tasks drop by 30-40% compared to a single-wall kitchen.</li>
  <li><strong>Open-plan friendly:</strong> One side stays open, making the kitchen feel part of the living or dining space — critical in apartments where walls make rooms feel cramped.</li>
  <li><strong>Scalable:</strong> Works for kitchens as small as 55 sq.ft. (7×8 ft) and as large as 180 sq.ft. (12×15 ft). The layout adapts; only the counter length changes.</li>
  <li><strong>Budget flexibility:</strong> An L-shape can be built from ₹1.2 lakh (laminate, basic hardware) to ₹8+ lakh (acrylic, Hettich, quartz top) depending on materials and accessories.</li>
</ul>
