<h2>The Work Triangle &amp; Zone Planning</h2>
<p>The "work triangle" — the path between your stove, sink, and refrigerator — determines how efficient your kitchen feels. In an L-shape layout, the triangle naturally falls into a compact right-angle configuration. Here is how to plan each zone.</p>
<h3>The Five Kitchen Zones</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Consumables Zone (Pantry):</strong> Dry storage, spice racks, and pull-out larder units. Place this near the refrigerator for logical flow — you retrieve ingredients from both cold and dry storage simultaneously.</li>
  <li><strong>Non-Consumables Zone (Dishes):</strong> Plates, glasses, serving bowls, and cutlery. Place this adjacent to the dishwasher or drying rack, and close to the dining area for easy table-setting.</li>
  <li><strong>Cleaning Zone (Sink):</strong> Sink, dustbin, cleaning supplies, and dishwasher. Position this with plumbing access in mind — moving plumbing is the single most expensive change in a kitchen renovation.</li>
  <li><strong>Preparation Zone (Counter):</strong> The largest uninterrupted counter space. This is where you chop, mix, and assemble. Ensure at least 24 inches of clear counter between the sink and stove.</li>
  <li><strong>Cooking Zone (Stove/Hob):</strong> The hob, chimney/hood, and adjacent counter for hot pans. Keep this away from the window (wind disrupts gas flames) and away from the fridge (heat damages the compressor).</li>
</ol>
