<h2>L-Shape Designs for Small Kitchens (55-80 sq.ft.)</h2>
<p>A small kitchen does not mean a compromised kitchen. In fact, the L-shape is at its most efficient in compact spaces because the work triangle becomes tight — you can reach everything without taking more than two steps.</p>
<h3>8 Rules for Small L-Shape Kitchens</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Go light:</strong> White or light wood-tone uppers make the walls recede. Never use dark colours on uppers in a small kitchen.</li>
  <li><strong>Use slab doors:</strong> Flat, handle-less cabinet doors with J-profile or G-profile channels eliminate visual clutter. No protruding handles in tight spaces.</li>
  <li><strong>Extend uppers to ceiling:</strong> Every inch of vertical space matters. Crown moulding at the ceiling line gives a finished, built-in look.</li>
  <li><strong>Choose a single-bowl sink:</strong> A large single-bowl sink (24×18 inches) is more practical than a double-bowl in small kitchens — you gain counter space.</li>
  <li><strong>Under-counter appliances:</strong> Built-in microwave, under-counter oven, and integrated dishwasher keep the counter clear.</li>
  <li><strong>Glass-front uppers:</strong> One or two glass-front upper cabinets break the visual monotony and make the kitchen feel more open.</li>
  <li><strong>Continuous backsplash:</strong> One material from counter to ceiling (tiles or back-painted glass) creates an unbroken visual plane that elongates the wall.</li>
  <li><strong>Under-cabinet lighting:</strong> LED strips under upper cabinets eliminate shadows on the counter and make the kitchen feel twice as bright.</li>
</ol>
