<h2>Appliance Placement Planning</h2>
<p>Where you place appliances in an L-shape kitchen determines whether the layout flows smoothly or fights you at every step.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Hob/Cooktop:</strong> Centre of the long arm, with at least 12 inches of counter on each side. Never at the very end of an arm (no landing space for hot pans). Always directly under the chimney/hood.</li>
  <li><strong>Chimney:</strong> Mount 24-28 inches above the hob. Auto-clean models save maintenance. Size: 60 cm for 3-burner hob, 90 cm for 4-burner.</li>
  <li><strong>Refrigerator:</strong> End of the short arm or at the junction entry point. The fridge door should open away from the L, not into the work triangle.</li>
  <li><strong>Microwave:</strong> In a tall unit at elbow height (36-48 inches from floor), or mounted under the upper cabinet. Never on the countertop — it wastes 3-4 sq.ft. of prep space.</li>
  <li><strong>Dishwasher:</strong> Adjacent to the sink on either side, built-in under the counter. Plumbing connections shared with the sink make installation simple and cost-effective.</li>
  <li><strong>Water purifier:</strong> Wall-mounted above or near the sink, inside an upper cabinet with a cut-out back panel for the water line.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Lighting Design for L-Shape Kitchens</h2>
<p>Kitchen lighting is a three-layer system. Skipping any layer creates shadows, eye strain, or a flat atmosphere.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Layer 1 — Ambient (ceiling):</strong> Flush-mount LED panels or recessed downlights providing general illumination. Target 300-500 lux across the room. Warm white (3000K) for a cosy feel; neutral white (4000K) for a crisp, modern look.</li>
  <li><strong>Layer 2 — Task (under-cabinet):</strong> LED strip lights or puck lights under every upper cabinet. These eliminate the shadow your body casts when you face the counter. Non-negotiable for safe, comfortable food preparation.</li>
  <li><strong>Layer 3 — Accent (decorative):</strong> Inside glass-front cabinets, above open shelves, or a pendant light over a breakfast bar. These create visual depth and make the kitchen feel like a designed space rather than a utility room.</li>
</ul>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span><strong>Budget hack:</strong> If you can afford only one lighting upgrade, install under-cabinet LED strips. At ₹800-2,000 for the entire kitchen, it is the single highest-impact, lowest-cost improvement you can make. It transforms how the kitchen looks and how comfortable it is to cook in.</div>
