<h2>12 Common L-Shape Kitchen Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Ignoring the work triangle:</strong> Placing the stove and sink on opposite arms forces you to cross the kitchen for every task. Keep them on the same arm with 24-36 inches between them.</li>
  <li><strong>Wasting the corner:</strong> A standard blind corner cabinet wastes 40% of the space. Invest in a carousel, magic corner, or Le Mans unit.</li>
  <li><strong>Too few electrical outlets:</strong> Plan for at least 6-8 outlets at counter height. Every appliance (mixer, toaster, kettle, food processor) needs one. Retrofitting outlets after installation is expensive and ugly.</li>
  <li><strong>Placing the hob under a window:</strong> Wind disrupts gas flames, curtains are a fire hazard, and grease splatters on the glass. Keep the hob on a solid wall.</li>
  <li><strong>All dark cabinets in a small kitchen:</strong> Dark colours absorb light and make small rooms feel smaller. If you want dark tones, limit them to the lower cabinets.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping a backsplash:</strong> The wall behind the hob and sink gets hit with oil, water, and turmeric daily. A proper backsplash (tile, glass, or acrylic) is essential — paint is not sufficient.</li>
  <li><strong>Choosing handles that catch clothing:</strong> D-handles and bar handles snag on saree pallus, dupattas, and apron strings. Consider J-profile (integrated groove) or push-to-open mechanisms.</li>
  <li><strong>Forgetting ventilation:</strong> A chimney handles cooking fumes but the kitchen also needs cross-ventilation or an exhaust fan for heat removal — especially in Indian cooking with deep frying and tadka.</li>
  <li><strong>Using the same countertop height everywhere:</strong> The ideal hob counter height is 34 inches, but the prep area is more comfortable at 36 inches. A 2-inch height variation between zones reduces back strain during long cooking sessions.</li>
  <li><strong>Over-specifying tall units:</strong> A tall pantry unit looks impressive but eats 2 feet of wall space. In a small L-shape kitchen, one tall unit is the maximum — two will make the kitchen feel like a corridor.</li>
  <li><strong>Skipping soft-close hinges:</strong> Regular hinges slam and loosen over time. Soft-close hinges add ₹50-80 per door but eliminate the number one hardware complaint in Indian kitchens.</li>
  <li><strong>Not planning for the dustbin:</strong> A pull-out dustbin unit under the sink is non-negotiable. Freestanding bins block floor space, create tripping hazards, and look untidy.</li>
</ol>
